{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgimQKkdwBLZzfQW2to7V","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Bn3ciTbJru2JFxj2t9RFz2","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"working":"The line opens with clef, three sharps and cut time. Next comes a repeat sign and a single 8th rest before the first barline. That rest is bar 37, the 8th-rest pickup after the repeat.\n\nFirst full bar: 8th rest (marked pp), then a 16th pair, B4 A4. A grace note leads to G4 8th, then G4 A4 16ths, a dotted B4 with D5 16th, D5 8th and an 8th rest. This is bar 38.\n\nSecond bar: the same figure again, which is bar 39.\n\nThird bar, under \"Cresc:\": a slurred rising-and-falling run of 8ths, F4 G4 A4 B4 A4 G4, then F4 notes. This is bar 40.\n\nLast bar: four beamed 16ths on F4, which write out the tremolo quarter. Then a 16th group that includes a sharp (E#4) and comes down to E4. A custos ends the line. This is bar 41.\n\nNothing contradicts the edition's pitches or rhythms.","right":true,"insights":"The two identical bars (38 and 39) and the crescendo bar with its run of 8ths made it easy to place the line. The hard part was the tiny 8th-rest pickup after the repeat sign, which could be taken for a number. A clearer image of the start of the line, or a bar-37 entry in the edition that mentions the repeat sign, would have made that bar easier to confirm."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5436,"cache_read_input_tokens":523,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5436},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ece2a13a-2e3b-4cfc-bccd-ef710f056629","uuid":"1f018113-4588-40d8-9849-9d192c1fd823","timestamp":"2026-1